# CleanNest — response headers.
#
# Consumed by Cloudflare Pages (project "cleannest", build: `npm run build`,
# output dir "."). GitHub Pages ignores this file and serves it as a static
# text file, which is harmless.
#
# Note: production HTML is served through the `cleannest-redirects` Worker,
# which proxies to https://cleannest.pages.dev and returns that response
# untouched — so these headers reach real visitors. Redirect responses (301s)
# are generated by the Worker itself and do not carry them; that is expected.

# Content-hashed build output: the filename changes whenever the bytes change,
# so it is safe to cache hard. Without this, Pages answers every request with
# `cache-control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate`, forcing every visitor to
# re-validate every stylesheet and script on every page view.
#
# Deliberately NOT applied to /assets/img/* or /assets/fonts/* — those files are
# not content-hashed and get replaced in place, so they must keep revalidating.
/assets/generated/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# Site-wide security headers. HTML keeps its default revalidation (it changes
# on every deploy).
/*
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=15552000; includeSubDomains
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN
